1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,408
The school Week Three outline joins peripheral IV, tibial IO and timed
twelve-lead acquisition. Its Wednesday discussions use prolonged
vomiting with difficult veins and chest pressure with previous
myocardial infarction.

2
00:00:15,408 --> 00:00:27,792
This lab uses new cases with different ages and evolving observations
to rehearse those objectives. These are not recovered school test
questions or an answer key to the Simulation Channel.

3
00:00:27,792 --> 00:00:41,928
Before a planned first intervention, ask what else could explain the
findings. After each intervention, name what changed, what should have
changed and what got worse. Say the reasoning so that a partner can
challenge it.

4
00:00:41,928 --> 00:00:54,552
These pauses are brief cognitive checks, not permission to delay
immediate lifesaving care. Simulation practice and a completed reading
panel do not replace the actual school device checkoffs.

5
00:00:54,552 --> 00:01:10,440
A forty-six-year-old adult has vomited for three days and taken very
little fluid. The pulse is one hundred eighteen, blood pressure
ninety-two over fifty-eight and respiratory rate twenty-four. The
hands are cool and sitting up produces dizziness.

6
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:22,944
Begin with the primary assessment and immediate support. Determine
whether vomiting threatens the airway, whether breathing is effective
and whether the circulation findings are persistent and credible.

7
00:01:22,944 --> 00:01:37,680
A saturation of ninety-seven percent does not demonstrate adequate
tissue oxygen delivery. Flow, hemoglobin and tissue demand still
matter; repeat patient assessment instead of declaring the circulation
normal.

8
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:49,968
The small veins create a technical challenge, but the clinical
question comes first: what indicated treatment needs access now, who
is authorized to provide it and what can continue while access is
prepared?

9
00:01:49,968 --> 00:02:04,416
Vomiting supports volume loss but does not explain its own cause. Ask
about abdominal pain, diarrhea, fever, blood, medicines, pregnancy
possibility where relevant and the sequence of symptoms.

10
00:02:04,416 --> 00:02:19,896
Consider metabolic disease, infection, bleeding and cardiac illness as
the findings justify. Do not recite an enormous differential that
changes nothing; identify the important alternative and what evidence
would make you act differently.

11
00:02:19,896 --> 00:02:31,440
Check glucose when indicated and reconcile the history with the
examination. A person described as merely dehydrated may have an
illness requiring a different treatment or destination.

12
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:45,744
State a working explanation and its uncertainty. For example: volume
depletion is likely, but I am checking for a metabolic or bleeding
cause while maintaining support and preparing indicated access.

13
00:02:45,744 --> 00:02:58,944
Assign a clinician to the patient rather than to the needle. That
person maintains observations, responds to vomiting or deterioration
and keeps conveyance moving while another person prepares access.

14
00:02:58,944 --> 00:03:11,856
Explain the route, purpose and relevant discomfort, and obtain the
applicable consent. Check allergy, relevant access restrictions and
the reason the chosen limb and vein are suitable.

15
00:03:11,856 --> 00:03:25,776
Prepare the actual catheter, connection, flush and securement so that
a successful insertion can immediately become a protected usable
system. Missing equipment encourages contamination and interruption.

16
00:03:25,776 --> 00:03:37,032
Agree on when attempts stop and help is requested. Follow the actual
policy for attempt limits; do not import an invented universal number
from a different device or service.

17
00:03:37,032 --> 00:03:50,592
On the trainer, identify the proposed vessel before exposing the
sharp. Describe its course and usable segment, then palpate for depth,
compressibility and refill using the trained method.

18
00:03:50,592 --> 00:04:02,280
A visible blue line alone is not sufficient identification, and a
nearby pulse belongs in your assessment. Do not insert into an
uncertain structure because the patient needs a quick result.

19
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:15,264
Match the vein to the actual catheter and therapy, preserving distal
options when suitable. A poor distal vein is not automatically the
right choice merely because distal first is a useful general
principle.

20
00:04:15,264 --> 00:04:29,256
If the vein collapses or the landmarks remain uncertain, stop and
reassess. Positioning, appropriate assistance and an alternative
authorized plan are safer than repeatedly fishing through tissue.

21
00:04:29,256 --> 00:04:41,688
Use the complete peripheral workflow taught earlier in the week.
Maintain hand hygiene, appropriate protection, the required skin
preparation and drying, and a no-touch approach to critical parts.

22
00:04:41,688 --> 00:04:55,416
Use the actual catheter instructions and school-supervised technique.
Flashback, threading and safety activation differ between products, so
a remembered angle or movement from another catheter is not enough.

23
00:04:55,416 --> 00:05:09,840
If the prepared site or connection is contaminated, identify the break
and restore the appropriate clean or aseptic state before proceeding.
Do not narrate an ideal sequence while physically continuing a
contaminated one.

24
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:22,176
Confirm function using the authorized method, secure the system and
assess during actual delivery. A catheter in the skin is not proof
that the prescribed treatment has entered the circulation.

25
00:05:22,176 --> 00:05:34,752
During the simulated flush the patient reports pain and swelling
appears around the site. Stop delivery and assess the patient,
connection and local tissues rather than forcing more volume through
resistance.

26
00:05:34,752 --> 00:05:50,016
Distinguish a local access complication from a systemic reaction or an
unrelated change. The response depends on the actual substance and
injury; an irritant or vesicant problem requires its specific
escalation pathway.

27
00:05:50,016 --> 00:06:04,128
Do not automatically flush a suspected harmful extravasation or remove
every device before checking the relevant plan. Some substance-
specific management may use the existing access for aspiration or
other treatment.

28
00:06:04,128 --> 00:06:16,632
Account for the actual amount delivered and any uncertainty. Restore
patient support and choose the next appropriate route or help; the
station is not passed simply because you once saw flashback.

29
00:06:16,632 --> 00:06:29,688
After an authorized simulated treatment, compare the same meaningful
observations with the baseline. Record the dose or volume actually
delivered and the time, not just the volume printed on the bag.

30
00:06:29,688 --> 00:06:42,816
Ask whether the intended perfusion target improved. A slightly higher
pressure without improved mentation or peripheral findings is
incomplete evidence and needs interpretation in the whole clinical
picture.

31
00:06:42,816 --> 00:06:56,808
Now add new breathlessness and crackles to the simulation. Reassess
immediately for treatment-related harm or another cause, stop or
modify the implicated treatment as indicated, and request appropriate
help.

32
00:06:56,808 --> 00:07:07,992
The correct response is not to finish a planned volume merely because
it was initially reasonable. A treatment remains conditional on the
patient and the current findings.

33
00:07:07,992 --> 00:07:20,760
The school asks when the difficult-vein patient might require tibial
IO and includes an awake-IO exercise. Learn that decision, but do not
treat the classroom scenario as unrestricted field authority.

34
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:36,912
The checked current PCP independent branch is restricted to patients
twelve or older in clearly hypovolemic cardiac arrest, with the stated
critical fluid and failed or unavailable peripheral-access conditions.
It is not a general dehydration pathway.

35
00:07:36,912 --> 00:07:50,640
The alternative described in the current procedure requires direct on-
scene supervision by an ACP or higher clinician. A phone conversation
alone does not become the on-scene supervision required by that
branch.

36
00:07:50,640 --> 00:08:05,448
Our awake patient therefore does not become independently eligible
merely because a vein failed. State the actual authority and
supervision before the simulated drill is prepared, and continue
necessary support and conveyance.

37
00:08:05,448 --> 00:08:20,808
Before the trainer is touched, identify the patient category and the
exact branch of authority. Current D zero three prohibits PCP
cannulation under twelve by any method; do not borrow a broader
manufacturer age range.

38
00:08:20,808 --> 00:08:35,232
Screen the proposed limb and site using the full procedure and actual
device instructions. Relevant fractures, local infection, previous
attempts and uncertainty about landmarks must be resolved before
insertion.

39
00:08:35,232 --> 00:08:48,192
The checked PCP pathway is tibial and permits two collective attempts,
not two attempts for every provider. A failed or dislodged site is not
simply recannulated as though nothing happened.

40
00:08:48,192 --> 00:08:58,944
Use the actual school device and supervised landmarks. This rehearsal
does not invent a needle length, insertion depth or pressure setting
for an unidentified product.

41
00:08:58,944 --> 00:09:11,016
The saved school case specifies zero point five milligrams per
kilogram of lidocaine over sixty seconds before fluid. That is the
source the student should recognize in the classroom discussion.

42
00:09:11,016 --> 00:09:27,216
The checked current adult PR twelve regimen instead specifies forty
milligrams over one hundred twenty seconds and a sixty-second dwell.
The authority is the relevant ACP pathway or the permitted PCP under
direct on-scene ACP supervision.

43
00:09:27,216 --> 00:09:42,192
For arithmetic only, the school formula applied to sixty kilograms
gives thirty milligrams. That calculation does not establish a
complete regimen, an allowed maximum, product suitability or
permission to administer it.

44
00:09:42,192 --> 00:09:55,968
At the station, identify which approved protocol sheet the instructor
is using before preparing simulated medication. Do not create a hybrid
by combining the school weight-based dose with the current timing or
the reverse.

45
00:09:55,968 --> 00:10:08,304
Use the actual procedure to verify placement and function before the
planned delivery. A needle that feels stable or a patient who feels
less pain is not by itself sufficient confirmation.

46
00:10:08,304 --> 00:10:21,408
Monitor the limb and site during treatment, including pain, swelling,
leakage and relevant distal findings. Secure the system so that moving
the patient does not turn a working route into a displaced one.

47
00:10:21,408 --> 00:10:37,536
If abnormal swelling or severe pain raises concern for extravasation
or compartment injury, stop the implicated delivery and obtain the
appropriate urgent review. Do not compensate for poor flow by
increasing pressure without reassessment.

48
00:10:37,536 --> 00:10:52,368
Hand over the route, site, attempt count, insertion time, actual
medication and fluid delivered, response and complications. The
receiving team needs the full route history, not simply IO present.

49
00:10:52,368 --> 00:11:05,256
A fifty-nine-year-old adult has twenty-five minutes of central
pressure and nausea. There is a previous infarction, and the person
says the sensation is similar. They are alert, cool and sweaty.

50
00:11:05,256 --> 00:11:19,272
Assess and support the patient while promptly preparing a diagnostic-
quality twelve-lead. A prior infarction raises concern, but the
history still needs its timing, associated symptoms and relevant
medicines.

51
00:11:19,272 --> 00:11:32,688
Ask what else could explain the presentation, including important
dangerous alternatives when the findings suggest them. The question
widens attention; it does not erase the need to act on likely acute
coronary disease.

52
00:11:32,688 --> 00:11:45,384
A previous tracing can help if promptly available, but searching for
it must not delay current acquisition or indicated care. A normal-
looking early trace also does not exclude acute coronary syndrome.

53
00:11:45,384 --> 00:11:56,448
Explain why the chest electrodes and exposure are necessary, obtain
appropriate consent and protect privacy with the least exposure
compatible with correct placement.

54
00:11:56,448 --> 00:12:10,320
Prepare the skin and locate the anatomical landmarks. Position breast
tissue respectfully when needed; do not place the precordial
electrodes over breast tissue simply to avoid a clear explanation.

55
00:12:10,320 --> 00:12:22,728
One provider remains responsible for the patient and urgent indicated
care while another acquires the tracing. If the patient deteriorates,
announce the change and reprioritize together.

56
00:12:22,728 --> 00:12:35,640
Use actual patient identification and time on the real clinical trace.
Training names and a simulated clock belong only to the trainer and
must not become false clinical documentation.

57
00:12:35,640 --> 00:12:51,744
Saved school seven G defines the target: a diagnostic-quality trace
acquired and readable in under three minutes from patient contact, in
a cooperative appropriate patient. The actual device checkoff and
grading rubric remain separate source gaps.

58
00:12:51,744 --> 00:13:05,280
Locate V one and V two in the fourth interspaces on the right and left
sternal borders. Locate V four in the fifth interspace at the
midclavicular line, then V three between V two and V four.

59
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:18,120
Place V five and V six horizontally level with V four at the
appropriate anterior and midaxillary lines. Do not follow the downward
slope of a rib or estimate the entire set by nipple position.

60
00:13:18,120 --> 00:13:32,664
Complete the limb connections and verify identity, signal quality and
calibration. A fast unusable tracing does not meet a quality
acquisition goal; note the reason for delay and repeat practice
safely.

61
00:13:32,664 --> 00:13:45,864
The monitor suddenly displays a very rapid irregular-looking pattern
while the patient is shaking. Look at the person, assess pulse and
perfusion, and inspect the signal rather than treating the screen
alone.

62
00:13:45,864 --> 00:14:00,600
Compare simultaneous leads for recognizable underlying complexes, and
check electrode contact, cables and motion. Artifact may coexist with
real disease, so correcting it does not finish the clinical
assessment.

63
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:15,024
If the person becomes unresponsive and has no definite pulse, follow
the arrest pathway without a prolonged troubleshooting delay.
Uncertainty about one tracing is not a reason to ignore a changed
patient category.

64
00:14:15,024 --> 00:14:29,808
When the patient is perfusing, correct the identified problem and
reacquire a usable trace, preserving and explaining relevant
originals. Do not label a dangerous rhythm artifact simply because the
patient was initially awake.

65
00:14:29,808 --> 00:14:42,744
Use the structured method from the seven G lessons: describe rate,
regularity, atrial activity, the P-to-QRS relationship, QRS width and
other relevant intervals before naming a rhythm.

66
00:14:42,744 --> 00:14:57,696
For the diagnostic trace, examine contiguous lead patterns and the
clinical context. The device interpretation is a prompt for review,
not an independent diagnosis or authority to bypass the current
pathway.

67
00:14:57,696 --> 00:15:12,696
Acute coronary disease may evolve or be present without a classic
initial ST-elevation pattern. Repeat when symptoms or the condition
change, and obtain the required expert interpretation and destination
guidance.

68
00:15:12,696 --> 00:15:26,736
An extra lead set or an alternative position must be accurately
labelled and communicated. Do not silently replace a standard
electrode position and then interpret the result as an unchanged
standard twelve-lead.

69
00:15:26,736 --> 00:15:39,840
While you compare leads, the patient becomes pale and nearly faints.
The pulse feels weaker and the repeat blood pressure is substantially
lower. Stop treating interpretation as the only task.

70
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:54,600
Reassess airway, breathing, circulation and consciousness, confirm the
observations promptly and provide indicated support. Announce the
change so the crew does not continue the previous stable-patient plan.

71
00:15:54,600 --> 00:16:07,728
Request appropriate advanced help and follow the current pathway for
the actual rhythm and clinical state. A treatment requiring additional
authority does not become independently allowed because the situation
is frightening.

72
00:16:07,728 --> 00:16:20,352
If arrest occurs, move immediately to the arrest pathway. If a pulse
remains, maintain the appropriate pulse-present plan and avoid
assuming that every abnormal ECG requires a shock.

73
00:16:20,352 --> 00:16:32,112
Rehearse the assigned two-step blood-pressure method with a correct
cuff and supported arm. A palpatory estimate helps guide the later
inflation and avoid missing an auscultatory gap.

74
00:16:32,112 --> 00:16:45,168
Compare a reliable pulse with the electrical rate. An electrical
complex does not guarantee an effective stroke volume, and a weak
irregular peripheral pulse may not equal every detected QRS.

75
00:16:45,168 --> 00:16:57,528
Use pulse oximetry with signal quality and patient context. Poor
perfusion and other limitations can affect the result; a plausible
normal value does not rule out low flow or anemia.

76
00:16:57,528 --> 00:17:10,440
Record observations at meaningful times with posture, treatment and
relevant device settings. Changing the method without saying so can
create an apparent improvement that is only measurement variation.

77
00:17:10,440 --> 00:17:25,824
For a simulated D ten label stating ten grams per one hundred
millilitres, twelve point five grams corresponds to one hundred
twenty-five millilitres. Show grams cancelling rather than jumping
from a drug name to a memorized volume.

78
00:17:25,824 --> 00:17:41,544
For three hundred millilitres over ninety minutes, divide by one point
five hours to obtain two hundred millilitres per hour. With a
simulated twenty-drop-per-millilitre set, the gravity calculation is
approximately sixty-seven drops per minute.

79
00:17:41,544 --> 00:17:57,360
Read the actual tubing factor and product concentration; neither
follows from the bag size or the colour of the set. Gravity delivery
varies, so verify actual flow and patient response rather than
trusting one count indefinitely.

80
00:17:57,360 --> 00:18:11,688
These are original arithmetic exercises, not recommended treatment
amounts for either case. Real administration still requires the
correct patient, indication, authorized dose, route and reassessment
plan.

81
00:18:11,688 --> 00:18:23,880
Use SBAR for consultation or the required receiving-service structure.
Lead with the active problem and urgency rather than making the
listener reconstruct them from a long chronological story.

82
00:18:23,880 --> 00:18:38,472
For the access case, include the route and site, attempts, any failed
or displaced access, actual fluid or medication delivered and the
patient response. Do not imply that the full prepared volume entered.

83
00:18:38,472 --> 00:18:53,760
For the chest case, include symptom onset, relevant history, serial
observations, acquisition times, important ECG findings and any
change. Identify any nonstandard electrode position or extra lead set.

84
00:18:53,760 --> 00:19:08,112
State the next specific need and confirm the receiver understood.
Documentation should preserve the same facts, sequence, uncertainty
and response; a polished handover must not hide a complication.

85
00:19:08,112 --> 00:19:20,616
At the peripheral station, the partner observes the full chain and
introduces one contamination or local-failure cue. A technically
successful insertion is only one part of the performance.

86
00:19:20,616 --> 00:19:33,912
At the tibial station, verbalize authority, exclusions, attempt
counting and the approved pain protocol before using the supervised
trainer. Do not practice invasive procedures on yourself or a partner.

87
00:19:33,912 --> 00:19:48,216
At the ECG station, rehearse the school contact-to-readable-trace
target and check the complete electrode map. Add movement artifact
after the first acquisition so that quality correction is observed
rather than only described.

88
00:19:48,216 --> 00:20:01,320
At the integrated station, change one clinical finding after an
intervention. Require a revised plan and structured handover, not a
repeated memorized script that ignores the new information.

89
00:20:01,320 --> 00:20:13,752
Describe what actually happened, including a missed cue or a helpful
action. Avoid calling a learner careless or naturally talented when
the important evidence is a specific step and its timing.

90
00:20:13,752 --> 00:20:26,688
Ask what information the learner had at the time and why the action
seemed reasonable. This distinguishes a knowledge gap, a misleading
cue, a device problem and a failure to re-evaluate.

91
00:20:26,688 --> 00:20:41,136
Connect the step to its clinical consequence. For example, everyone
preparing IO left vomiting unsupported, or a rapidly printed ECG was
unusable because the precordial landmarks were guessed.

92
00:20:41,136 --> 00:20:53,808
Choose one correction, repeat the relevant sequence and record whether
the correction was observed. A positive discussion without a changed
performance is not evidence that the skill is now reliable.

93
00:20:53,808 --> 00:21:07,656
Use the physiology and reading lessons to explain why the decisions
work: pressure is not flow, electrical activation is not guaranteed
mechanical output, and infused water does not stay entirely in plasma.

94
00:21:07,656 --> 00:21:20,712
Then demonstrate the complete sequence with the actual supervised
training equipment. Explain the intervention, protect the route,
verify function and reassess the patient after real or simulated
delivery.

95
00:21:20,712 --> 00:21:36,216
All eleven assigned Week Three reading rows now have paired
explanatory guides in this course. Exact school editions, some linked
activities, original PPT bodies and assessed station materials still
require source confirmation.

96
00:21:36,216 --> 00:21:48,312
This is preparation, not a claim that the whole school week is
certified complete. Bring unresolved device and protocol differences
to the instructor, and keep the actual school assessment your own
work.
